### What gap did Sequenced Collections fill?

Before Java 21, order was a concept scattered across types with no shared interface. A `List` gave its first element with `get(0)` and its last with `get(size() - 1)`. A `Deque` had `getFirst()` and `getLast()`. A `LinkedHashSet` had a defined iteration order but no direct way to get the last element except iterating to the end. A `LinkedHashMap` and a sorted map had order too, but accessing the ends was inconsistent.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class BeforeSequencedCollections {

    public void inconsistentAccess() {
        // List: index arithmetic
        List<String> accounts = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "SAV2002");
        String first = accounts.get(0);
        String last = accounts.get(accounts.size() - 1); // error-prone

        // Deque: different methods
        Deque<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>(accounts);
        String dFirst = queue.getFirst();
        String dLast = queue.getLast();

        // LinkedHashSet: no direct last-element access
        Set<String> recent = new LinkedHashSet<>(accounts);
        String sFirst = recent.iterator().next();
        // No getLast()! Must iterate to the end
    }
}
```

### What are the new interfaces and methods?

`SequencedCollection` is the base, adding uniform first and last access. `SequencedSet` extends it for unique elements. `SequencedMap` is the map counterpart.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SequencedCollectionDemo {

    public void demonstrateNewMethods() {
        // SequencedCollection: getFirst, getLast, reversed
        List<String> transactions = new ArrayList<>(
            List.of("TXN-001", "TXN-002", "TXN-003")
        );
        String oldest = transactions.getFirst();  // TXN-001
        String newest = transactions.getLast();    // TXN-003

        // SequencedSet: unique elements with order
        SequencedSet<String> recentAccounts = new LinkedHashSet<>();
        recentAccounts.addFirst("SAV2001");
        recentAccounts.addLast("CUR3001");
        String lastUsed = recentAccounts.getLast(); // CUR3001

        // SequencedMap: first and last entries
        SequencedMap<String, BigDecimal> balances =
            new LinkedHashMap<>();
        balances.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("50000.00"));
        balances.put("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("120000.00"));
        balances.put("SAV2002", new BigDecimal("75000.00"));

        Map.Entry<String, BigDecimal> first =
            balances.firstEntry();
        Map.Entry<String, BigDecimal> last =
            balances.lastEntry();

        System.out.println("First: " + first);
        System.out.println("Last: " + last);
    }
}
```

```text
First: SAV2001=50000.00
Last: SAV2002=75000.00
```

### How does the reversed() view work?

The `reversed()` method returns a view, not a copy. Iterating it walks the original in reverse, and for mutable collections, changes through the view write back to the original.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ReversedViewDemo {

    public void newestFirstStatement() {
        List<String> transactions = new ArrayList<>(
            List.of("Deposit 10000", "Transfer 5000", "Withdrawal 2000")
        );

        // View, not a copy: walks backwards
        List<String> newestFirst = transactions.reversed();
        for (String txn : newestFirst) {
            System.out.println(txn);
        }
        // Output:
        // Withdrawal 2000
        // Transfer 5000
        // Deposit 10000

        // Changes through the view write back
        newestFirst.set(0, "Withdrawal 3000");
        System.out.println(transactions.getLast());
        // Prints: Withdrawal 3000
    }
}
```

### Which existing types were retrofitted?

| Type | Now Implements |
|---|---|
| `List` | `SequencedCollection` |
| `Deque` | `SequencedCollection` |
| `LinkedHashSet` | `SequencedSet` |
| `SortedSet` / `NavigableSet` | `SequencedSet` |
| `LinkedHashMap` | `SequencedMap` |
| `SortedMap` / `NavigableMap` | `SequencedMap` |

Existing collections gained the new methods without changing their declared types.

### What are the edge cases and exceptions?

`getFirst()` and `getLast()` throw `NoSuchElementException` on an empty collection. `addFirst()` and `addLast()` on a sorted set or map throw `UnsupportedOperationException` because you cannot force a position against the comparator's order.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class SequencedEdgeCases {

    public void emptyCollectionAccess() {
        List<String> empty = new ArrayList<>();
        try {
            empty.getFirst(); // NoSuchElementException
        } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
            System.out.println("Empty list: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    public void sortedSetPositionConflict() {
        SequencedSet<String> sorted = new TreeSet<>();
        sorted.add("CUR3001");
        sorted.add("SAV2001");
        try {
            sorted.addFirst("ZZZ9999");
            // UnsupportedOperationException: cannot force position
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("Sorted set: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

### How is this different from using get(0) and get(size-1)?

| Operation | Before Java 21 | Java 21+ |
|---|---|---|
| First element (List) | `list.get(0)` | `list.getFirst()` |
| Last element (List) | `list.get(list.size() - 1)` | `list.getLast()` |
| First element (Set) | `set.iterator().next()` | `set.getFirst()` |
| Last element (Set) | Iterate entire set | `set.getLast()` |
| Reverse iteration | Copy + Collections.reverse | `collection.reversed()` |
| First map entry | Iterate to find | `map.firstEntry()` |

### What about SequencedMap-specific methods?

`SequencedMap` adds `firstEntry()`, `lastEntry()`, `pollFirstEntry()` (retrieves and removes), `pollLastEntry()`, `putFirst()`, `putLast()`, and `sequencedKeySet()`, `sequencedValues()`, `sequencedEntrySet()` for ordered views.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SequencedMapDemo {

    public void bankingMapOperations() {
        SequencedMap<String, BigDecimal> recentDeposits =
            new LinkedHashMap<>();
        recentDeposits.put("TXN-001", new BigDecimal("10000.00"));
        recentDeposits.put("TXN-002", new BigDecimal("25000.00"));
        recentDeposits.put("TXN-003", new BigDecimal("50000.00"));

        // First and last entries
        System.out.println("Earliest: " + recentDeposits.firstEntry());
        System.out.println("Latest: " + recentDeposits.lastEntry());

        // Poll removes and returns
        Map.Entry<String, BigDecimal> removed =
            recentDeposits.pollFirstEntry();
        System.out.println("Removed: " + removed);
        System.out.println("Remaining: " + recentDeposits.size());
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The feature adds no new data structures; it is purely a unifying API layered over existing ordered types. That is exactly why it is valuable: it removes a pile of index arithmetic and iterator boilerplate and makes ordered-collection code read the same everywhere. The reversed() view is a view, not a copy, which matters for performance with large collections.
